1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:06,236
Maeda and colleagues (2024) compare Nara wooden containers and wooden slips.

2
00:00:06,697 --> 00:00:09,645
Tree-ring dates are compared with written dates.

3
00:00:10,105 --> 00:00:13,776
Read how different sources constrain feature chronology.

4
00:00:14,237 --> 00:00:20,217
This study compares scientific dating and written evidence within archaeological contexts.

5
00:00:20,671 --> 00:00:25,250
Ask how independently the two types of evidence constrain the date and how their shared

6
00:00:25,263 --> 00:00:27,322
context is established.

7
00:00:27,776 --> 00:00:32,832
Follow the linked paper for its figures and detailed sample information.

8
00:00:33,635 --> 00:00:36,373
What event does the written year describe?.

9
00:00:36,677 --> 00:00:40,256
What outer wood is preserved in the ring-dated object?.

10
00:00:40,558 --> 00:00:44,749
Examine association, duration of use and redeposition.

11
00:00:45,203 --> 00:00:50,144
A written year also requires interpretation: what event was being recorded?

12
00:00:50,598 --> 00:00:55,433
Likewise, the meaning of an outer-ring date depends on what wood survives.

13
00:00:55,887 --> 00:01:00,677
Compare the meanings of the dates, not just whether the numerals match, and assess how the

14
00:01:00,690 --> 00:01:03,580
objects came to be associated.

15
00:01:04,383 --> 00:01:08,575
Connect images, measurement paths and numerical records.

16
00:01:09,030 --> 00:01:13,912
Check sample identifiers, context, species and preservation.

17
00:01:14,372 --> 00:01:18,773
Verify rather than assume correspondence with a paper's samples.

18
00:01:19,227 --> 00:01:24,892
Public data allow a reader to trace numerical measurements back to the inspected material.

19
00:01:25,346 --> 00:01:30,543
However, a similar site name does not establish that a database item and a paper use the

20
00:01:30,556 --> 00:01:32,030
same samples.

21
00:01:32,491 --> 00:01:38,648
Check identifiers and documentation before transferring interpretations between the two.

22
00:01:39,451 --> 00:01:43,610
Suppression may involve climate, insects or competition.

23
00:01:44,072 --> 00:01:48,802
Combine scars, recruitment, mortality and management records.

24
00:01:49,256 --> 00:01:52,907
Examine comparison trees and survivorship bias.

25
00:01:53,361 --> 00:01:55,894
Recall the Lesson 6 examples.

26
00:01:56,348 --> 00:02:01,355
Trees that survived and recovered may not represent the full impact on a forest.

27
00:02:01,809 --> 00:02:07,341
Dead trees, management history and other evidence help compare explanations.

28
00:02:07,795 --> 00:02:13,980
A common growth pattern is useful evidence, but it still needs ecological context.

29
00:02:14,783 --> 00:02:18,679
State region, season, climate variable and units.

30
00:02:19,141 --> 00:02:23,272
Check calibration, verification and response stability.

31
00:02:23,733 --> 00:02:27,542
Test the links from local climate to broader social change.

32
00:02:27,996 --> 00:02:33,627
Using a reconstruction in historical research does not remove the original model's limits.

33
00:02:34,088 --> 00:02:38,548
Read independent verification as well as calibration performance.

34
00:02:39,009 --> 00:02:44,233
Preserve the stated seasonal and spatial target when comparing the curve with documentary or

35
00:02:44,246 --> 00:02:46,768
archaeological evidence.

36
00:02:47,571 --> 00:02:49,651
All examples are fictional.

37
00:02:50,113 --> 00:02:52,856
A building record mentions 1210.

38
00:02:53,310 --> 00:02:57,494
Read the last preserved rings and bark status of A, B and C.

39
00:02:57,613 --> 00:03:01,718
8 minutes: claims, limits and additional evidence.

40
00:03:02,179 --> 00:03:06,600
Suppose these timbers were recovered from different parts of the present building.

41
00:03:07,060 --> 00:03:10,790
Pause and consider how their dates relate to the written record.

42
00:03:11,244 --> 00:03:15,146
Avoid assigning exact felling years when bark is absent.

43
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:21,481
Keep repair, reuse, source interpretation and possible dating errors among the explanations

44
00:03:21,494 --> 00:03:23,036
to check.

45
00:03:23,839 --> 00:03:29,669
A: timber use after formation of the 1187 ring; felling unresolved.

46
00:03:30,131 --> 00:03:35,881
B: the 1199 ring formed; inspect season and interval before use.

47
00:03:36,341 --> 00:03:42,157
C: wood formed through 1213; reassess its relation to the 1210 record.

48
00:03:42,618 --> 00:03:47,907
If the 1213 dating is secure, that timber could not already have been used in the building

49
00:03:47,920 --> 00:03:49,302
in 1210.

50
00:03:49,762 --> 00:03:55,131
Later repair is one possible explanation, but the table does not establish it.

51
00:03:55,591 --> 00:04:00,960
Inspect structural position, joints, working traces, the documentary claim and dating

52
00:04:00,973 --> 00:04:02,496
quality.

53
00:04:03,299 --> 00:04:05,695
Measurement and crossdating error.

54
00:04:06,157 --> 00:04:09,591
Missing information from preservation and sampling.

55
00:04:10,051 --> 00:04:14,078
Alternative environmental and historical interpretations.

56
00:04:14,538 --> 00:04:18,709
Uncertainty is not always captured by one numerical interval.

57
00:04:19,170 --> 00:04:24,025
A ring can be dated securely while the amount of lost outer wood or the storage interval

58
00:04:24,038 --> 00:04:25,558
remains unknown.

59
00:04:26,012 --> 00:04:31,348
State which parts of the reasoning are well constrained and which remain unresolved.
